.TH SPDW-PURGE 1 "2026" "SPDW Purge" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
spdw-purge \- cyberpunk disk analyzer and cleaner
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B spdw-purge
[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
.br
.B spdw-purge \-\-list
.br
.B spdw-purge \-\-clean
\fIsafe\fR|\fIall\fR|\fIkey,key,...\fR [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
.br
.B spdw-purge \-\-top
\fIPATH\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no arguments the GUI starts (elevated with pkexec). The GUI has four tabs:
SCAN (tree, top files, treemap, file types), JUNK, APPS and FINDER.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-list
Measure and list every junk category.
.TP
.B \-\-clean WHAT
Clean \fIsafe\fR categories, \fIall\fR non-destructive ones, or a comma-separated list of keys.
Destructive categories (old kernels, MX snapshots, whole ~/.cache, privacy traces) must be named explicitly.
.TP
.B \-\-top PATH
Print the biggest folders, file types and files under PATH.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Never modify anything; log what would happen.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/spdw-purge/actions.log
Audit log of every delete / command executed (or simulated).
.SH AUTHOR
sirpips \(em SPDW Factory Lab
